Agent: That’s a meaningful project — and there are several records sources and research steps that commonly yield good information about WWII-era refugees and displaced persons. Below I’ll outline practical steps, the key records to look for (both in the U.S. and in Europe), and tips on how to request them.

What information to gather first
- Full name (including maiden name or any alternate spellings)  
- Date/place of birth (even approximate)  
- Any U.S. arrival details you already know (year, port, sponsoring relative/organization)  
- Naturalization date/place (if she naturalized)  
- Names of relatives, last known addresses, or anecdotes (camp name, ship name, town in Germany)  
Having as many details as possible will speed searches and reduce ambiguity.

US records and places to search
1. USCIS Genealogy Program (formerly NARA’s immigration files)  
   - Request immigration, naturalization, and alien files (A-files/immigrant case files). Useful if she naturalized or was processed by immigration.  
2. National Archives (NARA) — immigration and refugee records  
   - Passenger arrival (ship) manifests; visa and refugee admissions records; wartime refugee program files.  
3. U.S. Census & Social Security records  
   - Census entries (1940, 1950, etc.) and Social Security application (SS-5) often list birthplace, parents’ names, arrival year.  
4. Local county records/newspapers where she lived  
   - Obituaries, local news, naturalization ceremonies, church bulletins. These can include immigration stories and family contacts.  
5. State Department / Refugee admissions files  
   - For formal refugee resettlement programs (postwar), some files may exist in federal archives or the State Dept. archives.

European and international archives
1. International Tracing Service / Arolsen Archives (Bad Arolsen, Germany)  
   - Holds extensive displaced persons, concentration camp, and refugee records for Europe post‑WWII. You can submit a formal inquiry for individual records.  
2. US Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) — Collections and Databases  
   - Survivor and refugee databases, transport/DP camp information, oral history collections. Useful even if your relative wasn’t Holocaust‑persecuted directly (it covers broader wartime displacement).  
3. German civil and regional archives (Standesamt, local archives)  
   - Birth, marriage, and local residency records in the town she came from may survive; you’ll need the locality to request these.  
4. International Red Cross tracing service (ICRC / ARC)  
   - Historically handled tracing of missing/refugees; the American Red Cross also has tracing services and postwar records.

Key record types that often help reconstruct a refugee’s story
- Passenger manifests / ship lists (arrival to US; list sponsors, last residence)  
- Visa/immigration papers and sponsorship affidavits  
- Naturalization papers (often include arrival date/place and birth info)  
- Displaced Persons (DP) camp lists, IRO/UNRRA records, repatriation files  
- Arolsen/ITS entries (registration in DP lists; camp transfers)  
- Military or repatriation transport lists (if evacuated)  
- Local newspapers, obituaries, church records, cemetery records

How to request records / practical tips
- Start with U.S. records you can easily access: Social Security (SS-5 request), Ancestry/FamilySearch/NARA passenger lists, and county clerk for naturalization. Many passenger lists and census records are online and searchable.  
- For USCIS/immigration case files, use the USCIS Genealogy Program request form — expect a fee and processing time.  
- For ITS/Arolsen queries you’ll submit through their request process; they respond to family history searches (may require proof of relationship or ID).  
- Contact USHMM and the Red Cross tracing service for targeted inquiries about displaced persons.  
- If requesting German civil records, identify the exact town (Standesamt) and contact the office — some will respond to English requests, but policies and fees vary.

Searching strategies
- Try all spelling variants and include maiden names.  
- Cross-check: an SS-5, a passenger manifest, and a naturalization paper often corroborate each other (arrival date, sponsor, last residence).  
- Use family interviews: even small oral details (an old town name, camp nickname, ship name, or religion) can point you to the correct archive.

Costs and timelines
- Expect fees and wait times: U.S. federal requests and USCIS files can take weeks to months; ITS responses can also take time. Local registries in Europe may charge per record and require mailed requests.  
- Online databases (Ancestry, FamilySearch, Fold3) may require subscriptions but often speed initial searches.

When to get professional help
- If you hit dead ends or the paperwork becomes complex, a professional genealogist specializing in European or refugee research can be very effective (they know local archives, languages, and procedures).

Next steps I can help with right now
- Draft an email template you can use to contact USCIS, ITS, a German Standesamt, or a local archivist.  
- Suggest specific search keywords and variations to use on Ancestry/FamilySearch (based on the name info you provide).  
- Help you organize the facts you already have into a timeline and the next record requests to make.

If you want, tell me the great‑aunt’s full name (including any maiden/alternate names), approximate birth year and town in Germany (if known), and any U.S. arrival details you already have — I’ll outline the most targeted next requests and draft the messages you could send to the archives.
